This folder contains R routines to create unbalanced SiB4 fluxes.

The main program is runToMakeBetas.R.  
All other functions are called in the calculation process.

The input file runToMakeBetas.txt contains the user specifications
for making GPP/RESP multipliers that will create sinks. 
Currently, there are four different processes that are being
used to enhance GPP:
  -- CO2 Fertilization 
  -- N American Nitrogen Fertilization
  -- European Nitrogen Fertilization
  -- Boreal Forest Warming
And there is one process being used to reduce respiration:
  -- N American Forest Regrowth
For all five of these processes, factors setting their
strength are set in the input file.  

To run, you will need to specify the SiB4 file
containing the fluxes to be used to create the 
multipliers.  It assumes that a year
of fluxes is contained in this file.  
Currently, we used climatological 
monthly mean fluxes from 1998 through 2018. If the 
output is in monthly files, rather than containing
the full year of fluxes, the individual files
can be joined together using the 'conjoin' routine
in the tools_post directory.

You will also need to specify the name of the 
output file being created that holds the multipliers,
a brief description of the output, and the 
source of the fluxes.  These last two are included
as attributes in the output netcdf file.

Finally, you will need to specify whether to save
the image of the mean annual sink (in umol/m2/s).
If it is being saved, specify the name of the
image (which is currently a png file).

When running the program, print statements show
the strength of the annual sink.  
--Please keep in mind the fluxes for crops are 
imbalanced due to harvest.  This missing 
respiration is included in crop_resp, but is 
not taken into account in these calculations.  To
compensate for this, the original sink from the
crop imbalance is shown, as is the new sink, 
and then the net imbalance showing the difference
(once crop_resp is applied).

To use these fluxes, multiply the SiB4 
assimilation ('assim') by the betaGPP and the
SiB4 respiration ('resp_bal') by the 
betaRESP.  For this version with fires, we used
the unbalanced respiration ('resp_tot').

To run, first you need to edit all options
in the runToMakeBetas.txt file.  Then to run it
I use rstudio.  Open that up and open the main file
(runToMakeBetas.R).  Make sure that file gets sourced,
then type
>runToMakeBetas()



